<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Portable CloudVault E2EE crypto core for the OpenBurnBar Windows port
    (Phase 2 · data & sync / cloud). This is the PLATFORM-INDEPENDENT C# port of
    the zero-knowledge CloudVault crypto that the macOS/iOS app and the
    apps/console browser client already share:

      * escrow key-wrap   — P-256 ECDH + HKDF-SHA256 + AES-256-GCM
        (a vault key wrapped by a trusted native device unwraps here and vice-versa)
      * sealText/openText  — AES-256-GCM detached (nonce/ciphertext/tag), v1 (no AAD)
        + v2 (path-bound AAD)
      * sealBlob/openBlob   — AES-256-GCM combined + vault-keyed plaintext-HMAC integrity
      * sealPayload/openPayload — AES-256-GCM combined + metadata-bound AAD
      * recovery wrap       — HKDF-SHA256(recovery key) + AES-256-GCM
      * vault-keyed HMAC     — blob-integrity / session-body / session-chunk / project-memory hashes
      * vaultKeyID / SHA-256 helpers

    Parity source (macOS, canonical):
      OpenBurnBarCore/Sources/OpenBurnBarCore/SharedModels/CloudVaultCrypto.swift
      OpenBurnBarCore/Sources/OpenBurnBarKernel/Platform/PlatformSupport.swift  (PlatformCrypto)
    Browser mirror:
      apps/console/lib/escrow.ts
    KAT vector (Apple CryptoKit origin, self-verified on emit):
      windows/tests/cloudsync/Fixtures/cloudvault-kat-vectors.json
      (generated by windows/cloudsync/tools/CloudVaultVectorGen)

    The Windows C# port must reproduce those vector bytes BOTH directions
    (windows/tests/cloudsync). Crypto invariants preserved EXACTLY: fail-closed on
    any tag / KDF / AAD mismatch; E2EE; the server never sees the vault key or
    plaintext.

    AES-256-GCM / HKDF-SHA256 / HMAC-SHA256 / SHA-256 come from the .NET BCL
    (System.Security.Cryptography) — identical on the macOS authoring host and on
    Windows CNG. P-256 ECDH uses the pure-managed, cross-platform
    BouncyCastle.Cryptography package (the SAME approved dependency the
    Computer-Use core already ships) so the ephemeral scalar can be pinned for the
    seal-direction KAT and the X-coordinate agreement is byte-identical to
    CryptoKit / WebCrypto / .NET ECDiffieHellman (proven in the test suite).

    Targets net8.0 so the net8.0-windows adapter + WinUI app can reference it,
    while the net10.0 test project consumes it too.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <RootNamespace>OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.Crypto</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.Crypto</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../app/OpenBurnBar.App.Configuration/OpenBurnBar.App.Configuration.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../../../crates/openburnbar-domain-core/bindings/csharp/OpenBurnBarDomainCore.Ffi/OpenBurnBarDomainCore.Ffi.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OpenBurnBar.CloudSync.Crypto.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
